GAD.reg("lesson","aud.nature.limits",{"id":"aud.nature.limits","title":"Inherent limitations of an audit","md":"## In plain words\n\n### The ghost potato supplier\n\nJune 2026. Tapri's internal auditor finds that all through 2025-26 the samosa factory paid \"Shree Ganesh Aloo Traders\" for potatoes that never arrived. The stores in-charge stamped each bill \"goods received\"; the purchase accountant, his partner in the scheme, passed it; the money went by bank transfer to a GST-registered firm in the accountant's cousin's name. In May, Iyer & Associates had signed an unmodified report on the 2025-26 accounts.\n\n> **Kesar:** Lakshmi-ji, you audited us! How did you miss a fake potato supplier?\n> **Lakshmi:** Every bill had your stores stamp, a valid GST number and a bank payment, Kesar-ji. Two of your own people agreed to lie together.\n> **Bunty:** Ma'am, I even ticked six of those bills. They looked perfect.\n> **Lakshmi:** That is what a well-planned fraud looks like, Bunty.\n\nDid the audit fail? Not necessarily. Every audit has limits built into it, its **inherent limitations**, and that is why it gives reasonable assurance and never absolute assurance. There are five:\n\n1. **The accounts rest on management's judgments** and on controls with weaknesses of their own: two employees acting together can defeat the best control.\n2. **Audit procedures have practical and legal limits.** The auditor tests samples, cannot force anyone to hand over information and is no expert in forged documents, while a determined fraud is built to look genuine.\n3. **An audit is not an investigation:** no power to search premises or take statements on oath.\n4. **Information loses relevance with time**, and checking everything would cost more than it is worth.\n5. **Nobody can see the future.** A business healthy today may fail tomorrow.\n\n::: analogy Airport security\nSecurity staff screen every bag, but open only some by hand (test checks). If two airline staff at the gate agree to wave someone through, the gate check fails (collusion). A good forgery fools an officer who is not a document expert (fabricated evidence). Security is not a police raid: it cannot search homes or take statements on oath (not an investigation). Checks must end before the flight leaves (timeliness and cost). And no one at the gate can promise a smooth flight (future events). Airports are much safer for security; they are not risk-free.\n:::\n\n::: key\nThe limitations are built into every audit, so audit risk can never be reduced to zero and the assurance is reasonable, not absolute. They explain why a properly planned and performed audit can still miss a misstatement; they never excuse an audit that was not properly performed.\n:::\n\n## How it works\n\n### 1. What \"inherent\" means here\n\nThe process of audit suffers from certain **inbuilt limitations** due to which an auditor cannot obtain an absolute assurance that financial statements are free from misstatement due to fraud or error (SM 1.14). In SA 200's words, the auditor is not expected to, and cannot, ==reduce audit risk to zero because there are inherent limitations of an audit== (SM 1.16). They result in most of the audit evidence on which the auditor draws conclusions and bases the opinion being **persuasive rather than conclusive** (SM 1.40). The objective they limit is in [[aud.nature.sa200|SA 200]]; audit risk itself is in [[aud.risk.model|the audit risk lesson]].\n\n::: diagram The five inherent limitations (SA 200)\n{\"type\": \"layers\", \"layers\": [{\"label\": \"1. Nature of financial reporting\", \"note\": \"preparation involves judgment by management\"}, {\"label\": \"2. Nature of audit procedures\", \"note\": \"practical and legal limits on obtaining evidence\"}, {\"label\": \"3. Not an investigation\", \"note\": \"not an official investigation into alleged wrongdoing\"}, {\"label\": \"4. Timeliness and relevance\", \"note\": \"value of information falls with time; reliability versus cost\"}, {\"label\": \"5. Future events\", \"note\": \"events or conditions may affect the entity adversely\"}], \"caption\": \"In ICAI's order (SM 1.14–1.17). Together they rule out absolute assurance.\"}\n:::\n\n### 2. The five factors, in ICAI's order\n\n#### (1) Nature of financial reporting\n\n**SA 200:** the preparation of financial statements involves judgment by management (SM 1.16).\n\nPreparing financial statements involves many judgments by management, which may involve subjective decisions or a degree of uncertainty, so the auditor may not be able to obtain absolute assurance that they are free from material misstatement due to fraud or error. One of the premises of an audit is that management acknowledges its responsibility for preparing the statements in accordance with the applicable framework and for devising suitable internal controls; but such controls may not have operated to produce reliable information, because of their own limitations. ICAI's example: a control requires every purchase bill to carry the stamp and signature of an authorised person in the \"Goods Receiving Section\", with the date and time the goods were received, so that only bills for goods actually received are paid. If the accountant and that authorised person collude, the control is overridden; such probable collusion is a limitation of internal control itself (SM 1.14).\n\n::: diagram How collusion defeats a good control\n{\"type\": \"seq\", \"w\": 380, \"lanes\": [\"Stores\", \"Accounts\", \"Auditor\"], \"events\": [{\"lane\": \"Stores\", \"text\": \"stamps a fake bill\"}, {\"from\": \"Stores\", \"to\": \"Accounts\", \"text\": \"bill marked \\\"received\\\"\"}, {\"lane\": \"Accounts\", \"text\": \"passes it, pays\"}, {\"from\": \"Accounts\", \"to\": \"Auditor\", \"text\": \"bill, stamp, bank entry\"}, {\"lane\": \"Auditor\", \"text\": \"tests a sample\"}, {\"note\": \"every check the control asks for is met\", \"over\": [\"Stores\", \"Auditor\"]}], \"caption\": \"The control needs two honest people. When the two collude, every document the auditor examines looks valid: the nature of financial reporting and the nature of audit procedures limit the audit together.\"}\n:::\n\n#### (2) Nature of audit procedures\n\n**SA 200:** there are practical and legal limitations on the auditor's ability to obtain audit evidence, such as the possibility that management or others may not provide, intentionally or unintentionally, the complete information relevant for preparation and presentation of the financial statements; and fraud may involve sophisticated and carefully organised schemes (SM 1.16).\n\nThe Study Material explains each limb (SM 1.14–1.15):\n\n- **Practical limitation:** an auditor does not test all transactions and balances; he forms his opinion by testing samples.\n- **Legal limitation:** management may not provide complete information as requested. There is no way by which the auditor can force management to provide it; if it is not provided, **he can only report**.\n- **Fraud by management:** management may consist of dishonest people, itself involved in fraud, concealing it by sophisticated and carefully organised schemes that are hard to detect, and may produce fabricated documents to make the evidence look valid. The auditor is **not an expert in authentication of documents**, so he may be led to accept invalid evidence on the basis of unauthentic documents.\n- **Related parties:** the entity may have entered into transactions with related parties that are only paper transactions; the auditor may not be aware of the relationships, or the procedures may not detect wrongdoing in such transactions ([[aud.evid.sa550|SA 550 related parties]]).\n\n#### (3) Not in the nature of investigation\n\n**SA 200:** an audit is not an official investigation into alleged wrongdoing (SM 1.16).\n\nThe auditor has no specific legal powers of search, or of recording statements of witnesses on oath, which an official investigation may need (SM 1.12). Hence he cannot obtain absolute assurance that the statements are free from material misstatement due to fraud or error (SM 1.15). The difference between an audit and an investigation is in [[aud.nature.scope|the scope of audit]].\n\n#### (4) Timeliness of financial reporting and decrease in relevance of information over time\n\n**SA 200:** the relevance of information, and thereby its value, tends to diminish over time, and there is a balance to be struck between the reliability of information and its cost (SM 1.17).\n\nThe auditor cannot verify each and every matter. ICAI's example: an auditor in his third year relies on information obtained during the second year's audit; something new may have happened, so the information is not timely and may have lost its reliability (SM 1.15–1.16).\n\n#### (5) Future events\n\n**SA 200:** future events or conditions may affect an entity adversely; adverse events may seriously affect its ability to continue its business (SM 1.16–1.17).\n\nThe business may cease to exist because of changes in market conditions, the emergence of new business models or products, or the onset of some adverse event. SA 570 adds that, as described in SA 200, the potential effects of inherent limitations on the auditor's ability to detect material misstatements are **greater for future events or conditions** that may cause an entity to cease to continue as a going concern: the auditor cannot predict them (SM 7.15; [[aud.complete.sa570|SA 570 going concern]]). Therefore, in view of all five factors, **an auditor cannot provide a guarantee** that the financial statements are free from material misstatements due to frauds or errors (SM 1.16).\n\n### 3. What the limitations never excuse\n\nA limitation explains a miss; it is not a licence to do less. The Study Material is explicit (for withheld information, the opinion then follows [[aud.report.sa705|SA 705]]):\n\n| The temptation | What the auditor must still do | SM |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"This procedure is difficult, slow or costly\" | As explained in SA 200, difficulty, time or cost is not in itself a valid basis to omit a procedure for which there is no alternative, or to be satisfied with evidence that is less than persuasive | 4.71 |\n| \"Controls look strong, so test less\" | Risk assessment is judgmental and internal control has inherent limitations, including management override: perform substantive procedures for each material class of transactions, account balance and disclosure, irrespective of assessed risks | 3.70 |\n| \"Documents are not my expertise\" | Records may be accepted as genuine unless there is reason to believe the contrary; where reliability is doubtful or fraud is indicated, investigate further and modify or add procedures | 11.13 |\n| \"They will not give me the papers\" | The auditor cannot force them; he reports, and SA 705 decides the opinion | 1.15 |\n| \"The future is not my job\" | The going concern responsibilities remain; but no reference to a material uncertainty is not a guarantee that the entity will continue | 7.15 |\n\nTwo sentences from ICAI's own report and engagement letter say the same thing, and both earn marks when quoted:\n\n- The risk of not detecting a material misstatement resulting from fraud is higher than for one resulting from error, as fraud may involve collusion, forgery, intentional omissions, misrepresentations, or the override of internal control (SM 8.18).\n- Because of the inherent limitations of an audit, together with the inherent limitations of internal control, there is an unavoidable risk that some material misstatements may not be detected, even though the audit is properly planned and performed in accordance with SAs (SM 11.19).\n\n## Worked examples\n\n:::: example Two misses, one excuse\nCA Vikram Rathore signed an unmodified report on the 2025-26 financial statements of Bhoomi Agro Exports Pvt Ltd, a supplier of Tapri's premix tea, on 28 May 2026. In September 2026 two matters come to light.\n\n(1) The fixed deposit of ₹4.5 crore shown in the balance sheet had been encashed in January 2026. The deposit receipt produced to CA Vikram was forged by the finance director, and the reply to the balance confirmation that CA Vikram had himself sent to the bank, and received directly from it, also confirmed the deposit: it had been signed by a branch officer who was part of the finance director's scheme.\n\n(2) An export receivable of ₹2.8 crore from a Dubai buyer, unpaid for 14 months at 31 March 2026, became irrecoverable when the buyer was declared bankrupt in August 2026. At the audit, the buyer's reply to CA Vikram's confirmation request had described the balance as \"disputed\", and he had accepted the CFO's oral assurance that it would be paid in full.\n\nThe board says the audit failed on both counts. CA Vikram replies that both are inherent limitations of an audit: \"a fraud designed to deceive me, and a future event\".\n\nExamine CA Vikram's reply with reference to the inherent limitations of an audit.\n\n::: answer Model answer\n**Relevant provision.** The process of audit suffers from certain inbuilt limitations, due to which an auditor cannot obtain an absolute assurance that financial statements are free from misstatement due to fraud or error. As per SA 200, the auditor is not expected to, and cannot, reduce audit risk to zero, because of inherent limitations arising from (1) the nature of financial reporting, (2) the nature of audit procedures, (3) the audit not being in the nature of an investigation, (4) the timeliness of financial reporting and the decrease in relevance of information over time, and (5) future events. Under the nature of audit procedures, fraud may involve sophisticated and carefully organised schemes, fabricated documents may be produced to make evidence look valid, and the auditor is not an expert in authentication of documents. Future events or conditions may affect an entity adversely after the audit. But the limitations do not excuse an audit that was not properly performed: the auditor may accept records and documents as genuine unless he has reason to believe the contrary, and in cases of doubt about the reliability of information the auditor shall investigate further and modify or add procedures; difficulty, time or cost is not a valid basis to be satisfied with audit evidence that is less than persuasive.\n\n**Facts of the case.** (1) The deposit was supported by a forged receipt and by a confirmation that the auditor controlled, falsified by collusion between the finance director and a bank officer. (2) At the reporting date the receivable was 14 months overdue and the debtor's own reply disputed it; the auditor relied on an oral assurance from management.\n\n**Analysis.**\n\n- *Matter (1)* falls within the inherent limitations. The scheme was sophisticated, carefully organised and involved collusion with a third party, so that both the document and the external evidence looked valid. CA Vikram, not being an expert in authentication of documents, had no reason to believe the receipt was not genuine, and he had kept control over the confirmation process. As an audit is not an investigation, he had no power to examine the bank officer on oath.\n- *Matter (2)* does not. The bankruptcy in August was a future event, but the misstatement it revealed existed at 31 March: the overdue period and the \"disputed\" reply were evidence, available at the audit, that the receivable might not be recoverable in full. An oral assurance from the CFO against that evidence is evidence less than persuasive, and the doubt about reliability required further investigation and further procedures. Calling it a \"future event\" does not cure an audit that did not respond to the evidence before it.\n\n**Conclusion.** CA Vikram's reply is justified for the fixed deposit, provided the audit was otherwise properly planned and performed in accordance with SAs: there is an unavoidable risk that such a fraud is not detected. It is not justified for the export receivable: that was a failure to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence, not an inherent limitation of the audit.\n:::\n::::\n\n:::: example Six notes from Chambal Bottlers\nCA Farah Siddiqui is auditing Chambal Bottlers Pvt Ltd, which bottles Tapri's iced tea, for 2025-26. Her planning notes say:\n\n(a) Management estimates the useful life of the new ₹12 crore bottling line at 15 years; the manufacturer's brochure gives \"10 to 20 years, depending on use\".\n\n(b) The company sold through 9,20,000 online orders during the year; the team will examine 120 of them.\n\n(c) The CFO will not share the billing system's change logs, calling them \"commercially sensitive\".\n\n(d) An anonymous e-mail alleges that the purchase head takes cash from a glass-bottle supplier; a director asks CA Farah to \"question the supplier's staff on oath and settle it\".\n\n(e) The team plans to value year-end inventory using the stock-ageing report prepared for its November interim visit.\n\n(f) A writ petition that could cancel the lease of the company's only factory site is pending in the High Court, and the judgment is not expected until after the audit report.\n\nIdentify the inherent limitation of audit that each note illustrates, and state in each case what the limitation does not excuse.\n\n::: answer Model answer\nThe process of audit suffers from inbuilt limitations due to which an auditor cannot obtain absolute assurance that the financial statements are free from misstatement due to fraud or error. The notes illustrate them as follows:\n\n| Note | Inherent limitation | What it does not excuse |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a) Useful life of 15 years | Nature of financial reporting: preparation involves judgments by management, with subjective decisions or a degree of uncertainty | Evaluating whether management's estimate is reasonable |\n| (b) 120 of 9,20,000 orders | Nature of audit procedures: a practical limitation. The opinion is formed by testing samples, not every transaction | Determining a sample size sufficient to reduce sampling risk to an acceptably low level (SA 530) |\n| (c) Change logs withheld | Nature of audit procedures: a legal limitation. The auditor cannot force management to provide information; he can only report | Considering the effect on the audit and the opinion under SA 705 |\n| (d) \"Question them on oath\" | Not in the nature of investigation: the auditor has no powers to record statements on oath | Treating the e-mail as an indication of possible fraud: investigating further and modifying or adding procedures |\n| (e) November ageing report | Timeliness and decrease in relevance of information over time: something new may have happened, so the information may have lost its reliability | Obtaining evidence as at the year end |\n| (f) Pending writ | Future events: future events or conditions may adversely affect the entity's ability to continue its business | Evaluating going concern under SA 570; the report is not a guarantee of the entity's future |\n\nBecause of these limitations the audit provides reasonable, not absolute, assurance; none of them permits the auditor to omit a necessary procedure or to accept evidence that is less than persuasive.\n:::\n::::\n\n## Under the hood\n\n### Why Bunty's 100% check would not cure it\n\nSuppose (an illustration, not Study Material data) that 12 of Tapri's 4,800 potato bills were fake and Bunty picked bills at random. The chart below shows the chance that at least one fake bill lands in his sample, computed from the hypergeometric distribution:\n\n::: diagram The chance that a random sample contains a fake bill\n{\"type\": \"plot\", \"h\": 290, \"x\": [0, 4800], \"y\": [0, 100], \"xlabel\": \"bills selected at random, of 4,800\", \"ylabel\": \"chance (%)\", \"legend\": \"none\", \"xticks\": [[0, \"0\"], [960, \"960\"], [1920, \"1,920\"], [2880, \"2,880\"], [3840, \"3,840\"], [4800, \"4,800\"]], \"yticks\": [0, 25, 50, 75, 100], \"series\": [{\"label\": \"at least one of 12 fake bills selected\", \"color\": \"a\", \"pts\": [[0, 0.0], [60, 14.0], [120, 26.2], [240, 46.0], [360, 60.8], [480, 71.8], [600, 79.9], [720, 85.8], [960, 93.2], [1200, 96.8], [1440, 98.6], [1680, 99.4], [1920, 99.8], [2400, 100.0], [2880, 100.0], [3360, 100.0], [3840, 100.0], [4320, 100.0], [4800, 100.0]]}], \"points\": [{\"x\": 60, \"y\": 14.0, \"label\": \"60 bills: 14%\", \"pos\": \"right\"}, {\"x\": 480, \"y\": 71.8, \"label\": \"480 bills: 72%\", \"pos\": \"right\"}], \"caption\": \"Twelve fake bills among 4,800, picked at random: the chance rises quickly with the sample, but it is certain only when 4,789 or more bills are picked.\"}\n:::\n\nSixty bills give a 14.0% chance of picking a fake one, 480 bills 71.8%, 960 bills 93.2% and 2,400 bills 99.98%. Certainty needs 4,789 bills (with only eleven left out, at least one fake must be in the sample), and checking all 4,800 at an assumed two minutes a bill takes 160 hours: 20 working days of 8 hours, for one account. And then the point of the potato fraud returns: a selected bill with a genuine-looking stamp, a valid GST registration and a bank payment is still not *recognised* as fake. Checking everything cures the sampling limitation only; collusion, fabricated documents, judgments and the future remain. That is why the balance between reliability and cost is itself one of SA 200's limitations.\n\n### Limitations of internal control are a different list\n\nThe Study Material also lists the inherent limitations of **internal control**: it gives only reasonable assurance; human judgment can be faulty; a control may be operated by someone who does not understand its purpose; controls can be circumvented by collusion or management override; management makes judgments on which controls to implement; and small entities may lack segregation of duties (SM 3.30–3.31; [[aud.risk.ic|internal control]]). They feed the first audit limitation (controls \"may not have operated ... due to their own limitations\"), but a question on the limitations of an **audit** wants the five factors above.\n\n## Exam angle\n\n::: exam Where it appeared, May 2024 to September 2026\nInherent limitations were examined in 9 of the new-scheme ICAI documents read for this lesson:\n\n- **Past paper:** May 2024 Q2(c) (3 marks: a pager plant shut down after mobile phones arrived; shareholders blame the auditor; \"List down the factors giving rise to the inherent limitations ...\"). The Suggested Answer gives all five factors with the Study Material's explanations.\n- **MTPs:** May 2024 Series I Q2(c) (3 marks: one example each of a practical and a legal limitation); May 2024 Series II Q4(c) (3 marks: the nature of financial reporting); May 2025 Series II Q5(c) (4 marks: \"not expected to, and cannot, reduce audit risk to zero ... Explain\", answered with SA 200's short form of the five); January 2026 Series II Q5(a) (5 marks: collusion between an accountant and the goods-receiving person).\n- **RTPs:** January 2025 Q9 (collusion, with SA 200's objectives); May 2026 Q9 (delayed documents, unsupported \"special procurement\", denied related parties, uniform-looking documents, test checks).\n- **Through other topics:** MTP September 2026 Series I Q5(c) (4 marks: going concern; the answer quotes SA 200 on the greater effect of inherent limitations for future events); the May 2026 paper, Q6(a) (5 marks: the auditor's responsibilities in an engagement letter, ending with the \"unavoidable risk\" sentence).\n- Old scheme: January 2021 Q1(b) (2 marks, correct or incorrect: \"the auditor is expected to reduce audit risk to zero\": incorrect).\n:::\n\n::: exam How the marks are earned\nMost Suggested Answers open with \"The process of audit suffers from certain inbuilt limitations due to which an auditor cannot obtain an absolute assurance ...\", then give the five headings in ICAI's order, each with its one-line explanation, and in a case map the facts to the headings. A 3-mark \"list\" wants all five; a case wants the two or three that fit, explained against the facts, and a conclusion.\n:::\n\n::: trick Five limitations in order\n\"**F**ine **A**uditors **N**ever **T**rust **F**utures\": Financial reporting, Audit procedures, Not an investigation, Timeliness, Future events. ICAI's order, so the answer reads the way the Suggested Answer does.\n:::\n\n## Answer frame\n\n### \"List / explain the inherent limitations of audit\" (3 to 4 marks)\n\n1. Opening: the process of audit suffers from inbuilt limitations, so absolute assurance is impossible; the auditor is not expected to, and cannot, reduce audit risk to zero (SA 200).\n2. The five headings in order, each with one line: judgment in financial reporting (and controls' own limits); practical and legal limits on evidence (samples, information withheld, sophisticated fraud); not an official investigation; relevance falls over time, reliability versus cost; future events.\n3. One closing line: hence the auditor cannot provide a guarantee.\n\n### A case (\"the auditor should have caught it\")\n\n**Provision** (opening + the relevant factors) → **facts** (what the control was, how it failed, what the auditor did) → **analysis** (map each fact to a factor) → **conclusion** (reasonable, not absolute, assurance; fault depends on whether the audit was properly planned and performed in accordance with SAs).\n\n::: cols\n**Loses marks**\n\n\"Audit is done on test check basis, so the auditor cannot detect all frauds.\"\n\nOne limitation, no SA 200, no facts.\n---\n**Passes**\n\nThe five headings listed correctly, but no explanation and nothing from the case.\n\nA list answer earns some marks; a case answer, little.\n---\n**Full marks**\n\nThe opening sentence, the five headings with their one-line explanations, the facts mapped (collusion, samples, documents), and a conclusion with the \"properly planned and performed\" condition.\n:::\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n::: mistake Hall of Shame: the internal-control list in an audit answer\nAsked for the inherent limitations of an audit, Bunty writes \"human judgment, lack of understanding, collusion, management override, small entities\". That is the list for **internal control** (SM 3.30–3.31). **Fix:** write the five audit factors of SA 200; mention control limitations only inside the first factor.\n:::\n\n::: mistake \"Cost is an inherent limitation, so I skipped the stock count\"\n**Fix:** as explained in SA 200, difficulty, time or cost is not in itself a valid basis to omit a procedure for which there is no alternative, or to be satisfied with evidence that is less than persuasive (SM 4.71). The cost limitation is about not verifying every matter, not about skipping necessary procedures.\n:::\n\n::: mistake \"An audit is not an investigation, so fraud is not my concern\"\n**Fix:** the objective covers material misstatement \"whether due to fraud or error\". \"Not an investigation\" refers to the audit's purpose and the auditor's lack of powers of search and examination on oath; doubts about documents or indications of fraud still require further investigation within the audit (SM 1.12, 11.13).\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n::: quiz Check yourself\nQ: Case scenario. Tapri estimates that 6% of the ₹14 crore owed to it by franchisees will not be collected. Depending on how the cafés trade next year, a reasonable estimate could lie anywhere from 4% to 9%, and no audit procedure can remove that uncertainty. Which inherent limitation of an audit does this illustrate?\n- [ ] Future events\n- [x] Nature of financial reporting\n- [ ] Not in the nature of investigation\n- [ ] Nature of audit procedures: a practical limitation\n> Preparation of financial statements involves many judgments by management, which may involve subjective decisions or a degree of uncertainty (SM 1.14): that is the nature of financial reporting. \"Future events\" is the tempting answer, but that limb is about events that may adversely affect the entity and its ability to continue its business, not the uncertainty inside an estimate.\n\nQ: The managing director refuses to give the auditor certain documents, and the auditor has no power to compel him. This is an example of:\n- [ ] A practical limitation on obtaining audit evidence\n- [x] A legal limitation on obtaining audit evidence\n- [ ] A limitation arising from future events\n- [ ] No limitation, because the auditor can seize the documents\n> SM 1.15: there is no way the auditor can force management to provide complete information; he can only report. That is ICAI's example of a legal limitation. Testing samples is the practical one.\n\nQ: Select all that apply. Which of the following are inherent limitations of an audit under SA 200?\n- [x] The preparation of financial statements involves judgment by management\n- [x] Fraud may involve sophisticated and carefully organised schemes\n- [x] An audit is not an official investigation into alleged wrongdoing\n- [ ] The difficulty, time or cost of a particular procedure\n- [ ] The auditor's lack of independence from the entity\n> The first three are in SA 200's list (SM 1.16). Difficulty, time or cost is not in itself a valid basis for omitting a procedure (SM 4.71). Independence is part of the very meaning of audit (SM 1.5): an auditor who is not independent should not be doing the audit at all, so it is no limitation of a properly conducted one.\n\nQ: Case scenario. Iyer & Associates signed Tapri's unmodified report for 2025-26 on 20 May 2026. Which of these later discoveries illustrates the inherent limitation \"future events\"?\n- [ ] In July, it emerges that ₹2 crore of premix counted at 31 March had been marked \"damaged\" on the count sheets but was valued at cost\n- [ ] In July, the auditors realise that a supplier confirmation they relied on had been obtained two years earlier\n- [x] In July, a cyclone floods the Paradip warehouse and destroys stock and machinery that were in good condition at 31 March\n- [ ] In July, it emerges that the CFO had hidden a related party relationship with fabricated documents\n> Only the cyclone is an event after the audit that adversely affects the entity (SM 1.16). The damaged premix was visible in evidence at the audit, so it is a miss, not a limitation. The old confirmation is the timeliness limitation, and relying on it is not excused. Fabricated documents and hidden related parties fall under the nature of audit procedures.\n\nQ: A bank in Singapore charges ₹40,000 for each balance confirmation. For Tapri's ₹6 crore deposit with it, Bunty proposes to accept a photocopy of the bank statement given by Tapri's finance team instead, because \"there is a balance to be struck between the reliability of information and its cost\". Is he right?\n- [ ] Yes: the balance between reliability and cost is an inherent limitation, so the cheaper evidence is enough\n- [ ] Yes, provided management also gives a written representation that the deposit exists\n- [ ] No, because a photocopy is never audit evidence\n- [x] No: difficulty, time or cost is not in itself a valid basis to be satisfied with audit evidence that is less than persuasive\n> SM 4.71, quoting SA 200: difficulty, time or cost is not in itself a valid basis to omit a procedure for which there is no alternative, or to be satisfied with audit evidence that is less than persuasive. A photocopy is evidence, only less reliable than the original or a confirmation received directly; the cost limitation is about not verifying every matter, not about accepting weaker evidence for a material balance.\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n- **Inherent limitations:** inbuilt limits of the audit process, due to which an auditor cannot obtain absolute assurance that the financial statements are free from misstatement due to fraud or error.\n- **SA 200:** the auditor is not expected to, and cannot, reduce audit risk to zero; most audit evidence is persuasive rather than conclusive.\n- **(1) Nature of financial reporting:** management's judgments; controls with their own limits (collusion at the goods-receiving stamp).\n- **(2) Nature of audit procedures:** practical (samples) and legal (cannot force information; can only report) limits; sophisticated fraud and fabricated documents; not an expert in authentication; paper related party transactions.\n- **(3) Not an investigation:** no powers of search or of statements on oath. **(4) Timeliness:** relevance falls over time; reliability versus cost. **(5) Future events:** the entity may be adversely affected later.\n- Hence no guarantee: there is an unavoidable risk that some material misstatements are not detected even in an audit properly planned and performed in accordance with SAs.\n- **Never an excuse:** difficulty, time or cost does not justify omitting a procedure with no alternative (SM 4.71). (Bunty's 20 working days of ticking would not have caught the potato man either.)"});
